          <h2 class="serif">Ellen White on the 1904 &ldquo;New Organization&rdquo;</h2>
          <div class="quote">
            <p>
              &ldquo;The Lord has declared that the history of the past shall be rehearsed as we enter upon the closing work. Every truth that He has given for these last days is to be proclaimed to the world. Every pillar that He has established is to be strengthened. We cannot now step off the foundation that God has established. <strong>We cannot now enter into any new organization; for this would mean apostasy from the truth.</strong>&rdquo;
            </p>
            <cite>&mdash; Ellen G. White, Manuscript 129, 1905 &bull; 2 Selected Messages, p. 390.1</cite>
          </div>
          <div style="background:rgba(220,38,38,0.1);border:1px solid rgba(220,38,38,0.35);border-radius:12px;padding:20px 22px;margin-top:16px;">
            <p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-size:clamp(1.3rem,2.8vw,1.65rem);font-weight:700;color:#fca5a5;margin:0 0 10px;line-height:1.3;">What &ldquo;NEW ORGANIZATION&rdquo; (#1SM204) is Ellen White talking about here in 1905?</p>
            <p style="color:#d3c39d;margin:0;line-height:1.82;font-size:0.97rem;">She wrote this in <strong style="color:var(--ink);">1905</strong> &mdash; one year after <strong style="color:var(--amber);">April 15, 1904</strong>, the date the <em>General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists</em> was incorporated in Washington D.C. The document evidence is on this page. Verify it in the 1905 Yearbook PDF above. The conclusion is unavoidable.</p>
          </div>
