        <section class="section">
          <h2 class="serif">1903 Committee Minutes &mdash; Premeditated from the Start</h2>

          <h3 style="color:var(--amber);font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-size:1.3rem;margin:16px 0 10px;">October 22, 1903 &mdash; Sixtieth Meeting: The Corporation Is Voted</h3>
          <p>At the <strong>Sixtieth Meeting of the General Conference Committee</strong>, Washington D.C., 9:45 A.M., October 22, 1903, the committee voted to create the new D.C. corporation and named <strong>seven original incorporators</strong>. The following is the verbatim record:</p>
          <div class="quote" style="margin-top:14px;">
            <p>&ldquo;Inasmuch as the General Conference Association is a corporation of the State of Michigan, requiring that its meetings shall be held in that State, to great inconvenience, as the headquarters is now removed; and,&mdash;</p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;">&ldquo;Inasmuch as the legal Foreign Mission Board is a corporation of New York, requiring its legal annual meetings to be held there; it is,&mdash;</p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;">&ldquo;Voted, That the General Conference Committee be instructed to create a corporation under the laws of the District of Columbia, to transact the business heretofore attended to by the Foreign Mission Board and the General Conference Association; and,&mdash;</p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;">&ldquo;Voted, That the Foreign Mission Board be wound up and its assets transferred to the new corporation; and further,&mdash;</p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;">&ldquo;Voted, That so far as possible to arrange, the new corporation shall give its notes to replace the notes of the Gen. Conf. Ass&rsquo;n., and that when the Michigan corporation lapses, its affairs be wound up.</p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;"><strong>&ldquo;Voted, That the incorporators be the following, and that they be instructed to elect themselves a Board to hold over until the next General Conference: A.G.Daniells, W.A.Spicer, W.T.Bland, W.W.Prescott, S.N.Curtiss, J.S.Washburn, A.P.Needham.&rdquo;</strong></p>
            <p style="margin-top:8px;">&ldquo;Voted, That we request the General Conference Association of Michigan and the Foreign Mission Board of New York as rapidly as possible to transfer all of their assets and liabilities to the new corporation.&rdquo;</p>
            <cite>&mdash; Sixtieth Meeting, General Conference Committee, Washington D.C., October 22, 1903</cite>
          </div>
          <div class="image-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin-top:16px;">
            <a href="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/10-22-1903-sixteth-meeting-general-confernce-committee-meeting-minutes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <img
                src="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/10-22-1903-sixteth-meeting-general-confernce-committee-meeting-minutes.jpg"
                alt="Sixtieth Meeting GC Committee &mdash; October 22, 1903 &mdash; Original Seven Incorporators Named"
                loading="lazy"
                style="border-radius:10px;border:2px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.35);"
              />
            </a>
          </div>
          <div class="links" style="margin-top:10px;">
            <a href="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/GCC1903-10-22-1903-sixteth-meeting-general-conference-committee-washington-dc.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" download>&#8659; Download October 22, 1903 Minutes PDF</a>
          </div>

          <h3 style="color:var(--amber);font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-size:1.3rem;margin:22px 0 10px;">April 13, 1904 &mdash; Five of Seven Replaced for D.C. Residency</h3>
          <p>By April 1904, five of the seven original nominees were no longer D.C. residents. The October 22, 1903 vote was reconsidered. Of the original seven, only <strong>A.G. Daniells</strong> and <strong>A.P. Needham</strong> survived. W.A. Spicer, W.T. Bland, W.W. Prescott, S.N. Curtiss, and J.S. Washburn were dropped &mdash; replaced by J.R. Scott, H.E. Rogers, and D.K. Nicola, who were D.C. residents.</p>
          <div class="quote" style="margin-top:14px;">
            <p>&ldquo;Attention was called to the fact that the majority of those named as incorporators at the October meeting of the Committee are not now residents of the District of Columbia, as required by law.&rdquo;</p>
            <cite>&mdash; GC Committee Minority Meeting, April 13, 1904</cite>
          </div>
          <div class="quote" style="margin-top:10px;">
            <p>&ldquo;VOTED, That inasmuch as the majority of the persons named in the action of October 22, 1903 are not now resident in the District of Columbia, we appoint A.G. Daniells, J.R. Scott, A.P. Needham, H.E. Rogers, D.K. Nicola as incorporators of the General Conference Corporation of Seventh-day Adventists.&rdquo;</p>
            <cite>&mdash; GC Committee Minority Meeting, April 13, 1904 &mdash; signed A.G. Daniells, Chairman; W.A. Spicer, Secretary</cite>
          </div>
          <div class="image-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;margin-top:16px;">
            <a href="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/1903minutesfor1904incorporation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <img
                src="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/1903minutesfor1904incorporation.jpg"
                alt="GC Committee Minority Meeting &mdash; April 13, 1904 &mdash; Incorporators Reconsidered"
                loading="lazy"
                style="border-radius:10px;border:2px solid rgba(212,165,55,0.35);"
              />
            </a>
          </div>
          <div class="links" style="margin-top:10px;">
            <a href="/images/1904-image-of-papacy/GCC1904-01minutes.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" download>&#8659; Download April 1904 GC Committee Minutes PDF</a>
            <a href="https://documents.adventistarchives.org/Minutes/Forms/AllItems.aspx?RootFolder=%2fMinutes%2fGCC&FolderCTID=0x012000F14CCE0E47CC244BB8EA93FE785ED8BE00941CF68C17217C4CA49DE1E876677255" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#93c5fd;">&#128193; All GC Committee Minutes &mdash; Adventist Archives</a>
          </div>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px;">
            This two-step documented record (October 1903 &rarr; April 1904) proves the incorporation structure was planned, approved, and in motion well before April 15, 1904. The final five signers were chosen for <em>legal</em> residency qualification &mdash; not spiritual office.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== 1896 WARNING — LETTER 81 — 8 YEARS BEFORE 1904 ===== -->
      <div class="new-section" style="margin-top:14px;">
        <div style="border-left:5px solid rgba(220,38,38,0.7);border-radius:0 18px 18px 0;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#1a0000,#0d0a06 50%,#090e1e);padding:36px 36px 36px 38px;">
          <p style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:5px 12px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(220,38,38,0.15);border:1px solid rgba(220,38,38,0.35);color:#f87171;font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;margin:0 0 18px;">Prophetic Warning Written in 1896 &mdash; Eight Years Before April 15, 1904</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-size:clamp(1.15rem,2.2vw,1.55rem);font-style:italic;color:rgba(254,243,199,0.93);line-height:1.8;margin:0 0 16px;">&ldquo;The same work that has been done in the past will be carried forward under the guise of the General Conference Association&hellip; That same work will be done again, and done with greater power, as it was in the days when the Papacy was in its strength.&rdquo;</p>
          <cite style="color:#f87171;font-size:0.88rem;font-weight:700;font-style:normal;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.07em;display:block;">&mdash; Ellen G. White, Letter 81, May 31, 1896 &bull; 1888 Materials, pp. 1558&ndash;1573</cite>
          <p style="color:#9ca3af;margin:14px 0 0;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.72;">Eight years before the April 15, 1904 incorporation, Ellen White warned that the <em>General Conference Association</em> would become the vehicle for carrying forward the same persecution the Papacy exercised in the Dark Ages. This warning was not written after the fact &mdash; she gave it <strong style="color:var(--ink);">eight years in advance</strong>. The 1904 corporate incorporation is the fulfillment of her prophecy in plain sight.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ===== GCB APRIL 3, 1901 — EGW "THAT IS PAST" ===== -->
      <div class="new-section">
        <div style="border-left:5px solid rgba(214,167,69,0.7);border-radius:0 18px 18px 0;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0d0a06,#090e1e);padding:36px 36px 36px 38px;">
          <p style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:5px 12px;border-radius:999px;background:rgba(214,167,69,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(214,167,69,0.35);color:var(--amber);font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.1em;margin:0 0 18px;">Ellen G. White &mdash; General Conference Bulletin, April 3, 1901, par. 25 &mdash; Three Years Before 1904</p>
          <p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',serif;font-size:clamp(1.15rem,2.2vw,1.55rem);font-style:italic;color:rgba(254,243,199,0.93);line-height:1.8;margin:0 0 16px;">&ldquo;O, my very soul is drawn out in these things! Men who have not learned to submit themselves to the control and discipline of God, are not competent to train the youth, to deal with human minds. It is just as much an impossibility for them to do this work as it would be for them to make a world. That these men should stand in a sacred place, to be as the voice of God to the people, <strong style="color:var(--amber);font-style:normal;">as we once believed the General Conference to be &mdash; that is past.</strong> What we want now is a reorganization. We want to begin at the foundation, and to build upon a different principle.&rdquo;</p>
          <cite style="color:var(--amber);font-size:0.88rem;font-weight:700;font-style:normal;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.07em;display:block;">&mdash; Ellen G. White &bull; General Conference Bulletin, April 3, 1901, par. 25</cite>
          <p style="color:#9ca3af;margin:14px 0 0;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.72;">Three years before the April 15, 1904 incorporation, Ellen White declared the General Conference had forfeited its standing as &ldquo;the voice of God.&rdquo; She called for building on &ldquo;a different principle.&rdquo; What came three years later was not a different principle &mdash; it was the same centralized power structure, now incorporated under federal law. <strong style="color:var(--ink);">&ldquo;That is past&rdquo; was her verdict. April 15, 1904 confirmed it.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>

